{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778378085571,"sessionID":"ses_1f067f91affenQPzTqXH6YPulT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_8iLkFLwtu1RkAEJyqtQLl9Ie","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \"**hypertrophic chondrocyte**\\n\\n**Improvements needed**\\n- link to Uberon term \\n- revise the outdated textual defintion - remove \\\"terminally differentiated\\\" as historically these cells were viewed as terminally differentiated cells - Modern genetic lineage tracing has confirmed that a significant portion of these cells survive, dedifferentiate into a progenitor state, and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts (bone-forming cells) and osteocytes (PMID: 35179487), (PMID: 41207902).\\n\\n**defs**\\nA chondrocyte that is part of the hypertrophic cartilage zone. This cell is significantly enlarged and characterised by high expression of type X collagen (COL10A1) in both humans and mice. It actively coordinates endochondral ossification by mineralising the extracellular matrix, attracting blood vessels via angiogenic signalling, and mediating the transition from cartilage to bone - often by transdifferentiating into an osteoblast rather than undergoing apoptosis.\\n\\nPMID: 25321476\\nPMID: 35179487\\n\\n**Comments** REPLACE WHAT ALREADY EXISTS \\n Despite historical descriptions as 'terminally differentiated,' hypertrophic chondrocytes survive and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts. Genetic lineage tracing shows that hypertrophic chondrocytes do not simply undergo apoptosis but cross the cartilage–bone boundary, dedifferentiate into skeletal stem/progenitor cells, and then redifferentiate into osteoblasts and osteocytes. Single-cell RNA-seq further indicates that these chondrocyte-derived SSPCs are a major source of trabecular osteoblasts and marrow adipocytes.\\n(PMID: 35179487)\\n(PMID: 41207902)\\n\\n**Logical def**\\nAdd SubclassOF \\n- part of some 'hypertrophic cartilage zone' @copilot please find ID for this term 'hypertrophic cartilage zone' using OLS_MCP. Once found and validated, import the term into CL using docs/Adding_classes_from_another_ontology.md as guidance\\n- capable of some 'endochondral ossification'\\n\\n**Other notes**\\nRemove present in taxon 'homo sa... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"hypertrophic chondrocyte - link to Uberon & improve definition\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3506\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \"**hypertrophic chondrocyte**\\n\\n**Improvements needed**\\n- link to Uberon term \\n- revise the outdated textual defintion - remove \\\"terminally differentiated\\\" as historically these cells were viewed as terminally differentiated cells - Modern genetic lineage tracing has confirmed that a significant portion of these cells survive, dedifferentiate into a progenitor state, and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts (bone-forming cells) and osteocytes (PMID: 35179487), (PMID: 41207902).\\n\\n**defs**\\nA chondrocyte that is part of the hypertrophic cartilage zone. This cell is significantly enlarged and characterised by high expression of type X collagen (COL10A1) in both humans and mice. It actively coordinates endochondral ossification by mineralising the extracellular matrix, attracting blood vessels via angiogenic signalling, and mediating the transition from cartilage to bone - often by transdifferentiating into an osteoblast rather than undergoing apoptosis.\\n\\nPMID: 25321476\\nPMID: 35179487\\n\\n**Comments** REPLACE WHAT ALREADY EXISTS \\n Despite historical descriptions as 'terminally differentiated,' hypertrophic chondrocytes survive and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts. Genetic lineage tracing shows that hypertrophic chondrocytes do not simply undergo apoptosis but cross the cartilage–bone boundary, dedifferentiate into skeletal stem/progenitor cells, and then redifferentiate into osteoblasts and osteocytes. Single-cell RNA-seq further indicates that these chondrocyte-derived SSPCs are a major source of trabecular osteoblasts and marrow adipocytes.\\n(PMID: 35179487)\\n(PMID: 41207902)\\n\\n**Logical def**\\nAdd SubclassOF \\n- part of some 'hypertrophic cartilage zone' @copilot please find ID for this term 'hypertrophic cartilage zone' using OLS_MCP. Once found and validated, import the term into CL using docs/Adding_classes_from_another_ontology.md as guidance\\n- capable of some 'endochondral ossification'\\n\\n**Other notes**\\nRemove present in taxon 'homo sa... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n  \"comments\": [],\n  \"title\": \"hypertrophic chondrocyte - link to Uberon & improve definition\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3506\"\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__issue_context__.json","time":{"start":1778378085103,"end":1778378085367}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0e0ce06a719d99af0169ffe5647cb881a0998446b094d43b86"}},"id":"prt_e0f9810b7001tRfPi6fKx6Pkf6","sessionID":"ses_1f067f91affenQPzTqXH6YPulT","messageID":"msg_e0f9807b6001dTXg4PDgqjcJvr"}}
